WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.463
This briefing comes from Book 10 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:08.435
Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone:
how the authorization boundary,

3
00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:12.775
not the product catalog, decides which
cloud features an agency can use,.

4
00:00:12.775 --> 00:00:16.812
I'll take four questions: why the
boundary decides what you can use,

5
00:00:16.812 --> 00:00:20.475
what has to change, who owns the
boundary, and how to get there.

6
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:24.127
The short version: treat the
boundary as a list you can read,

7
00:00:24.127 --> 00:00:29.580
not a perimeter you can only accept, and
evidence every choice in your own package.

8
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.530
Why does the authorization
boundary decide what you can use?

9
00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:38.630
Start with two agencies holding
the same Microsoft 365 license.

10
00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:42.935
One runs a commercial tenant and sees
per-location network assessments,

11
00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:45.805
informed network routing
and an Adoption Score.

12
00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:49.217
The other runs a GCC tenant
and sees none of them,

13
00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:53.592
because Microsoft documents
each one as unavailable there.

14
00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:57.304
Three decisions, made by three
parties, have to line up.

15
00:00:57.304 --> 00:01:01.363
FedRAMP certifies a specific
offering at a certification class,

16
00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:03.467
and the Marketplace is the record.

17
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.992
The provider publishes,
feature by feature,

18
00:01:05.992 --> 00:01:08.656
what each government
environment leaves out.

19
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:13.217
And the agency authorizes its own
system: its authorizing official accepts

20
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:17.417
the risk of the configuration selected
and the integrations enabled.

21
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:20.274
FedRAMP says an agency
should never create a

22
00:01:20.274 --> 00:01:23.980
standalone authorization to
operate for a cloud service.

23
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.630
What the agency can use is
the overlap of all three.

24
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.090
A feature with no citation is
unverified, not available.

25
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.890
Chapter 02 explains why the gap stays
invisible until a design depends on it.

26
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:44.103
Surfaces are shared: Intune in GCC is
the same instance as commercial Intune.

27
00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:47.714
Exclusions are scattered, one
sentence per feature page.

28
00:01:47.714 --> 00:01:51.363
Features move: Microsoft
retired Adoption Score's

29
00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:56.140
technology-experience categories
for every tenant in early 2026.

30
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.340
And absence doesn't announce itself.

31
00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.540
A detection whose input never arrives
looks exactly like a quiet day.

32
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.703
The book then corrects a
widely repeated example.

33
00:02:05.703 --> 00:02:09.390
Five features were said to be
silently missing from GCC.

34
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.755
Checked against Microsoft's
documentation, one is:

35
00:02:12.755 --> 00:02:17.003
the Locations view of network
connectivity in the admin center.

36
00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:22.652
Device Health Attestation and expedited
updates are GCC High and DoD gaps.

37
00:02:22.652 --> 00:02:26.290
Endpoint analytics isn't
a documented GCC gap.

38
00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:30.615
And Azure Arc is an Azure service,
in Azure's FedRAMP High scope.

39
00:02:30.615 --> 00:02:36.790
An unverified boundary story sends an agency
to build compensations it doesn't need.

40
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.452
The book models a boundary as any
surface across which four things change.

41
00:02:42.452 --> 00:02:46.703
It's the book's own model, not a
FedRAMP or Microsoft construct.

42
00:02:46.703 --> 00:02:51.091
Telemetry fidelity asks which
signals actually arrive on this side.

43
00:02:51.091 --> 00:02:54.029
In GCC, admin-center network connectivity,

44
00:02:54.029 --> 00:02:59.402
informed network routing and Adoption
Score are documented as unavailable.

45
00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:03.848
Identity authority asks who
a claim's issuer resolves to:

46
00:03:03.848 --> 00:03:10.278
GCC pairs with Entra ID in commercial Azure,
GCC High and DoD with Azure Government.

47
00:03:10.278 --> 00:03:15.466
Enforcement responsibility asks who must
enforce a control: without Microsoft's

48
00:03:15.466 --> 00:03:20.653
per-circuit feedback, path selection rests
on the agency's own SD-WAN measurements.

49
00:03:20.653 --> 00:03:23.274
Provenance classification asks whether a

50
00:03:23.274 --> 00:03:26.041
claim is still authoritative,
or only derived.

51
00:03:26.041 --> 00:03:28.965
Each dimension raises its own drift class.

52
00:03:28.965 --> 00:03:34.140
Detection is shipped for two classes,
partial for one and design only for two.

53
00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.187
And FedRAMP's definition
of federal customer data

54
00:03:37.187 --> 00:03:40.815
excludes the analytics and
telemetry a provider generates.

55
00:03:40.815 --> 00:03:43.690
No certification promises you a signal.

56
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.865
What changed at FedRAMP?

57
00:03:45.865 --> 00:03:46.903
Three things.

58
00:03:46.903 --> 00:03:50.340
First, services are now
certified, in Classes A to D.

59
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.208
FedRAMP's February 2026 notice says Class
C includes the current Moderate baseline

60
00:03:56.208 --> 00:03:59.645
and Class D the current
High, and that agencies may

61
00:03:59.645 --> 00:04:03.753
authorize at any security
category they deem appropriate.

62
00:04:03.753 --> 00:04:07.441
Second, the rules have no
provider authorization boundary.

63
00:04:07.441 --> 00:04:11.118
In the machine-readable ruleset,

64
00:04:11.118 --> 00:04:16.840
version 2026.10.13.01, that
phrase occurs zero times.

65
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.598
The word boundary appears eight times,
four of them as a tag on rule families:

66
00:04:21.598 --> 00:04:24.974
minimum assessment scope,
cryptographic module use,

67
00:04:24.974 --> 00:04:29.578
vulnerability detection and response,
and certification data sharing.

68
00:04:29.578 --> 00:04:32.690
Security category may vary by resource.

69
00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:38.140
Third, change is notified, not re-approved,
and the deadlines are in business days.

70
00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.852
Routine recurring changes need no notice.

71
00:04:40.852 --> 00:04:44.577
Adaptive changes, within 10
business days after finishing.

72
00:04:44.577 --> 00:04:49.016
Transformative changes, initial
plans 30 business days before.

73
00:04:49.016 --> 00:04:53.196
Adoption is mandatory on January 1, 2027,

74
00:04:53.196 --> 00:04:57.870
and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.

75
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.020
Chapter 04 reads GCC from
Microsoft's own documents.

76
00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.911
It's the worldwide service with
a published list of exclusions,

77
00:05:06.911 --> 00:05:08.483
and the list isn't a ladder.

78
00:05:08.483 --> 00:05:14.587
Network connectivity in the admin
center, Adoption Score, the Office 365

79
00:05:14.587 --> 00:05:20.794
CDN and informed network routing are
missing in GCC, GCC High and DoD alike.

80
00:05:20.794 --> 00:05:25.607
Windows release health and Excel Office
Scripts reach GCC and stop there.

81
00:05:25.607 --> 00:05:29.809
The largest documented loss is
the network connectivity layer:

82
00:05:29.809 --> 00:05:35.120
a daily assessment from 0 to 100 for each
location, with 80 as the healthy baseline,

83
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.846
and performance attributed
to named network providers.

84
00:05:38.846 --> 00:05:42.645
A GCC agency funds its
own measurement instead.

85
00:05:42.645 --> 00:05:49.197
On the Marketplace, read October
21, 2026, the GCC package, MSO365MT,

86
00:05:49.197 --> 00:05:55.870
is FedRAMP Certified at Class C,
Moderate, with 100 authorizations.

87
00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:00.070
A second package with the identical
name is In Process at Class D.

88
00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:05.208
But GCC High is already Class D, and
still lacks network connectivity.

89
00:06:05.208 --> 00:06:08.730
A higher class doesn't
restore an excluded feature.

90
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.993
The landing zone is where the
boundary stops being a diagram.

91
00:06:12.993 --> 00:06:15.963
Each workload landing zone
is a subscription placed

92
00:06:15.963 --> 00:06:18.529
in a management group
that carries its policy.

93
00:06:18.529 --> 00:06:22.733
The baseline hierarchy puts an
intermediate root under the tenant root,

94
00:06:22.733 --> 00:06:24.905
and that's where boundary policy goes.

95
00:06:24.905 --> 00:06:29.642
Platform groups hold security,
management, connectivity and identity.

96
00:06:29.642 --> 00:06:33.917
Landing zone groups hold corporate,
online and local workloads.

97
00:06:33.917 --> 00:06:39.280
Make Sandbox the default, so no new
subscription lands outside the boundary policy.

98
00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.971
Each boundary decision gets a
mechanism: the cloud choice;

99
00:06:42.971 --> 00:06:46.354
Allowed locations with Deny;
allowed resource types,

100
00:06:46.354 --> 00:06:49.276
checked against Microsoft's
audit-scope list;

101
00:06:49.276 --> 00:06:54.120
the FedRAMP regulatory compliance
initiative, which Microsoft calls only

102
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.580
a partial view; and exemptions,
Mitigated or Waiver, with an expiry date.

103
00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.555
Roll each policy out in audit mode first.

104
00:07:01.555 --> 00:07:06.305
One catch: Microsoft's accelerators
are supported only in public Azure.

105
00:07:06.305 --> 00:07:10.710
In Azure Government, the platform
team adapts the code by hand.

106
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.797
Chapter 06 makes the
address plan the join key.

107
00:07:14.797 --> 00:07:18.924
A scan finds what answered; the
IPAM records what was allocated,

108
00:07:18.924 --> 00:07:21.598
so every inventory reconciles against it.

109
00:07:21.598 --> 00:07:26.599
BOD 23-01 already asks for
automated discovery every

110
00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:31.373
7 days across the entire
IPv4 space the agency uses.

111
00:07:31.373 --> 00:07:34.747
In Azure the enterprise
IPAM stays the authority.

112
00:07:34.747 --> 00:07:36.535
Reserve the block there first.

113
00:07:36.535 --> 00:07:39.568
Mirror it into Virtual
Network Manager pools,

114
00:07:39.568 --> 00:07:42.760
entering ranges used
elsewhere as static CIDRs.

115
00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.635
Then assign an Azure Policy
that denies any virtual network

116
00:07:46.635 --> 00:07:50.122
without a pool allocation,
and reconcile by discovery.

117
00:07:50.122 --> 00:07:55.091
Virtual Network Manager isn't named in
Microsoft's FedRAMP audit-scope tables,

118
00:07:55.091 --> 00:07:58.684
so record it as a dependency
until coverage is confirmed.

119
00:07:58.684 --> 00:08:03.497
Build it as five layered modules,
each writing its state as evidence.

120
00:08:03.497 --> 00:08:05.672
And treat outside inputs as code.

121
00:08:05.672 --> 00:08:11.838
There's no separate GCC endpoint list: a
GCC tenant uses the Worldwide instance,

122
00:08:11.838 --> 00:08:16.370
updated monthly, with 30
days' notice of new endpoints.

123
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.658
Code anyone can apply from a
laptop isn't change control.

124
00:08:20.658 --> 00:08:25.525
Chapter 07 adds the front door: the
governed request into the landing zone,

125
00:08:25.525 --> 00:08:28.557
which Microsoft calls
subscription vending.

126
00:08:28.557 --> 00:08:34.858
The loop has six steps: request, approve,
plan, apply, reconcile and close.

127
00:08:34.858 --> 00:08:41.183
The requester can't approve their own
request, which is AC-5, separation of duties.

128
00:08:41.183 --> 00:08:45.158
The approver reviews the actual
plan before anything changes.

129
00:08:45.158 --> 00:08:47.259
And step 5 carries the most weight:

130
00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:52.739
the pipeline writes the address allocation
back to the enterprise IPAM and fails the

131
00:08:52.739 --> 00:08:55.741
run if the two disagree,
because an unreconciled

132
00:08:55.741 --> 00:08:58.669
block is shadow inventory
the moment it exists.

133
00:08:58.669 --> 00:09:01.558
The pipeline is inside
the boundary question too.

134
00:09:01.558 --> 00:09:03.894
Terraform state is sensitive data.

135
00:09:03.894 --> 00:09:09.775
On October 21, 2026, the Marketplace
had no listing for HCP Terraform; GitHub

136
00:09:09.775 --> 00:09:12.809
Enterprise Cloud was listed at Class B,

137
00:09:12.809 --> 00:09:16.508
and GitLab Dedicated for
Government at Class C.

138
00:09:16.508 --> 00:09:21.620
The default is a self-hosted runner
and state inside the agency's boundary.

139
00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:23.720
Who owns the boundary?

140
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.932
The rule is one decider per row.

141
00:09:25.932 --> 00:09:30.300
The authorizing official determines
the boundary, signs every documented

142
00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.182
exception and decides whether a
change requires reauthorization.

143
00:09:34.182 --> 00:09:38.476
Under SP 800-37, explicit risk
acceptance can't be delegated,

144
00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:43.995
and for federal agencies the role is
assigned to government personnel only.

145
00:09:43.995 --> 00:09:46.324
The system owner is the boundary owner:

146
00:09:46.324 --> 00:09:49.090
keeper of the boundary
record and the package,

147
00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:53.457
and the decider on placing a new
workload, because vending an unrecorded

148
00:09:53.457 --> 00:09:56.077
subscription is the
cheapest way to turn an

149
00:09:56.077 --> 00:09:58.770
authorized system into
an unauthorized one.

150
00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.203
The CISO sets control requirements,

151
00:10:01.203 --> 00:10:05.283
and the ISSO reads provider change
notices against the record.

152
00:10:05.283 --> 00:10:08.467
The platform team builds
the hierarchy, policy,

153
00:10:08.467 --> 00:10:12.795
vending and probe, but doesn't
decide whether a gap is acceptable.

154
00:10:12.795 --> 00:10:15.800
And the contract is a boundary decision:

155
00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:20.445
the GCC listing says customers
must buy a GCC SKU to be covered.

156
00:10:20.445 --> 00:10:24.060
Only a contracting officer
changes what the agency bought.

157
00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.285
How do you handle a
capability that's outside?

158
00:10:27.285 --> 00:10:31.674
NIST SP 800-37 names four
options: compensating controls,

159
00:10:31.674 --> 00:10:38.023
accepting greater risk, a more trustworthy
provider, or not obtaining the service.

160
00:10:38.023 --> 00:10:42.772
Build a register, one feature per row,
each with the provider's own statement.

161
00:10:42.772 --> 00:10:47.210
Only vendor-confirmed rows support a
claim to a provider or an assessor.

162
00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:50.388
Probe what actually works in
your tenant, on a schedule,

163
00:10:50.388 --> 00:10:53.072
testing for the output
rather than the switch.

164
00:10:53.072 --> 00:10:57.960
Where a gap is real, build an in-boundary
equivalent and say it's derived.

165
00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:59.310
Or take an exception.

166
00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:04.802
ExpressRoute for Microsoft 365 is authorized
only when a regulatory requirement

167
00:11:04.802 --> 00:11:06.871
mandates direct connectivity,

168
00:11:06.871 --> 00:11:11.010
and the book found no FedRAMP rule
or FISMA provision that does.

169
00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:15.160
Otherwise the authorizing official
signs a dated risk acceptance.

170
00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.760
The 2026 rules add four scope
arguments, each with a limit.

171
00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.077
And buy the capability, not the badge:

172
00:11:22.077 --> 00:11:26.600
Microsoft's Network Provider
Program is closed to new providers.

173
00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.029
The third answer is an alternative
path: a different certified

174
00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:34.850
service for the workload, with the
identity plane left where it is.

175
00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:37.337
Read the Marketplace,
not the announcement.

176
00:11:37.337 --> 00:11:39.925
Google Workspace is listed at Class D,

177
00:11:39.925 --> 00:11:44.100
while Gemini for Government is
a separate listing at Class B.

178
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:49.025
Which AI features sit inside which
certification is a question for the package.

179
00:11:49.025 --> 00:11:51.375
The public record shows both outcomes.

180
00:11:51.375 --> 00:11:57.267
In September 2025 a cabinet-level department
awarded a five-year agreement worth up

181
00:11:57.267 --> 00:12:02.570
to 89 million dollars to move more than
50,000 employees to Google Workspace,

182
00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:07.200
under a GSA agreement with a
temporary 71 percent price reduction.

183
00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:11.573
A military department went the
other way, ending about 150,000

184
00:12:11.573 --> 00:12:16.288
Google email licenses after an
audit found about 16 percent in use.

185
00:12:16.288 --> 00:12:19.513
Keep Entra ID as the
source: provision users,

186
00:12:19.513 --> 00:12:23.187
federate sign-in, apply
Conditional Access there.

187
00:12:23.187 --> 00:12:27.813
Then close the gaps: Google super
administrators sign in outside it,

188
00:12:27.813 --> 00:12:31.560
and Google's directory is
derived, so report drift.

189
00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:36.297
Finally, evidence the boundary
in the agency's own package.

190
00:12:36.297 --> 00:12:41.617
The agency never authorizes a cloud service;
it authorizes its system that uses one,

191
00:12:41.617 --> 00:12:45.934
and its plan should only contain
what the agency is responsible for.

192
00:12:45.934 --> 00:12:48.431
FedRAMP's Rev5 guidance is blunt:

193
00:12:48.431 --> 00:12:52.960
don't copy and paste provider
controls into the agency plan.

194
00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:58.071
Write the boundary down once, as a
boundary record the system owner keeps,

195
00:12:58.071 --> 00:13:02.074
and cite it everywhere: the
system description under PL-2,

196
00:13:02.074 --> 00:13:07.440
each external service under SA-9 with
its Marketplace package, class and SKU,

197
00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:12.040
each crossing under CA-3, the gaps
the agency will close on the POA&M,

198
00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:15.873
and each equivalent in an
OSCAL component definition.

199
00:13:15.873 --> 00:13:17.522
Know whose package you're reading.

200
00:13:17.522 --> 00:13:22.592
The GCC listing says the service leverages
Azure Government as its infrastructure,

201
00:13:22.592 --> 00:13:26.285
so the inheritance chain runs
through two provider packages.

202
00:13:26.285 --> 00:13:30.725
A compensating narrative takes four
sentences: what the control needs,

203
00:13:30.725 --> 00:13:35.165
what's unavailable with its citation,
what runs instead marked derived,

204
00:13:35.165 --> 00:13:38.010
and where the remaining gap is recorded.

205
00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.910
Appendix A turns all
of this into requests.

206
00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:46.124
Three habits make a request
hard to deflect: name one item,

207
00:13:46.124 --> 00:13:50.248
cite the basis with a link, and
ask for data, not documents.

208
00:13:50.248 --> 00:13:51.709
Eight requests follow.

209
00:13:51.709 --> 00:13:54.797
Publish the per-feature
exclusion list as data.

210
00:13:54.797 --> 00:13:57.259
Name the exception path for each feature.

211
00:13:57.259 --> 00:14:03.310
Restore the network observability layer
in GCC, or publish the specific obstacle.

212
00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:09.160
Explain the two same-named GCC packages,
and what Class D would and wouldn't change.

213
00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:13.210
List the agents and clients that
sit outside the certified offering.

214
00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:19.684
Provide a FedRAMP-compatible trust center
with programmatic access: on October 21,

215
00:14:19.684 --> 00:14:24.844
2026, only 110 of 781 Marketplace
listings had a trust-center entry,

216
00:14:24.844 --> 00:14:29.535
and none of the three Microsoft
365 government packages did.

217
00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:32.460
Deliver change notices as a JSON feed.

218
00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:35.985
And document the cryptographic
modules for each service.

219
00:14:35.985 --> 00:14:40.647
Five of the eight rest on obligations
the rules already place on providers.

220
00:14:40.647 --> 00:14:43.470
A documented no is evidence too.

221
00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.595
So, four decisions.

222
00:14:45.595 --> 00:14:51.066
Build the feature register on cited rows
only, one row per feature and environment,

223
00:14:51.066 --> 00:14:54.445
and drop or relabel anything
without a vendor page.

224
00:14:54.445 --> 00:14:58.611
Name the boundary owner: the authorizing
official decides the boundary,

225
00:14:58.611 --> 00:15:02.707
and the system owner keeps the record
and gates every new subscription.

226
00:15:02.707 --> 00:15:07.275
Enforce the boundary in the landing
zone: policy at the intermediate root,

227
00:15:07.275 --> 00:15:10.120
audit before deny, and
the governed front door.

228
00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.621
And evidence every response,
whether equivalent,

229
00:15:13.621 --> 00:15:17.720
exception or alternative path,
in the agency's own package.

230
00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:21.797
The next step needs no award: before
the next architecture review,

231
00:15:21.797 --> 00:15:26.706
list every cloud feature your designs
depend on, with its Marketplace package,

232
00:15:26.706 --> 00:15:30.632
its availability in your environment
and the page that says so.

233
00:15:30.632 --> 00:15:33.594
Then put Appendix A's
requests to your provider.

234
00:15:33.594 --> 00:15:37.595
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

235
00:15:37.595 --> 00:15:41.120
Book 10 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

236
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:41.940
Thank you.
